# Chat Implementation Workflow

- When work touches chat, first read `CHAT_BACKEND_DESIGN.md`.
- Treat that document as the current design record, not as implemented behavior.
- Discuss the relevant design topic with the user before implementing it.
- Work through chat decisions one topic at a time and explain them in plain language with a concrete example.
- Explain why the choice is needed, its trade-offs, failure behavior, industry terminology, and credible alternatives.
- Do not silently resolve anything listed as an open decision.
- Treat Socket.IO with WebSocket-only transport and its in-memory adapter as selected decisions.
- Do not add native `ws`, HTTP long polling, or Redis unless the user agrees to change the design.
- Implement only the backend scope the user has explicitly approved; frontend chat remains deferred until requested.
- Before each implementation stage, identify the exact design sections and files it affects.
- If repository evidence conflicts with the design note, explain the conflict before changing code.
- If an implementation must deviate from the design, obtain agreement and update the design note so it does not become stale.

## Required Review Checkpoints

- Data model, references, ownership, indexes, ordering, and pagination.
- Accepted-connection authorization and block/unmatch implications.
- Idempotency, transaction boundaries, race conditions, and recovery.
- HTTP versus WebSocket responsibilities and event contracts.
- Socket authentication, exact Origin validation, limits, heartbeat, expiry, cleanup, and backpressure.
- Sent, delivered, read, and unread semantics.
- Logging privacy, correlation, abuse limits, and error behavior.
- Single-process assumptions and the Redis/outbox boundary.
- Tests, load conditions, performance budgets, and measured results.

Do not claim production readiness until the approved checkpoints are implemented and verified.
